\page page_continuous_integration Continuous Integration (CI)

\tableofcontents

Peano uses [GitHub Actions](https://docs.github.com/en/actions) for continuous integration.
A workflow is a `.yml` file in `.github/workflows/`; GitHub runs it on its own
hosted runners whenever its trigger fires. You find the status of all runs on the
[Actions page](https://github.com/tobiasweinzierl/peano/actions) of the repository.

\note The build and test pipelines used to run on the LRZ GitLab on our own
      runners on COSMA. Their definitions (`.gitlab-ci.yml` and the files in
      `.continuous_integration`) are still in the repository, but they are not
      ported to GitHub Actions yet and are not executed there. The only
      workflow that currently runs on GitHub is the documentation workflow below.


## Documentation workflow

The file `.github/workflows/doxygen.yml` builds this website and publishes it at
https://peano-framework.github.io/peano/. It runs

\li on every push to the branch `p4`, and
\li on demand: open the *Actions* tab, pick *Doxygen documentation* and press *Run workflow*.

It consists of two jobs:

\li `build` runs on `ubuntu-latest`. It checks out the sources, installs
    `doxygen`, `graphviz` and `pandoc` through `apt` and `jupyter`/`nbconvert`
    through `pip`, and then performs the same steps that you run locally to
    build the documentation:
    ```
    python3 documentation/convert-jupyter-notebooks.py
    doxygen documentation/Doxyfile
    ```
    The notebook conversion turns the tutorial notebooks into Doxygen pages (see
    \ref page_tutorials_overview). Doxygen writes its output to `doxygen-html/html`,
    which the job uploads as a GitHub Pages artifact.
\li `deploy` publishes this artifact to GitHub Pages.

\note The repository settings have to select *GitHub Actions* as the source under
      *Settings > Pages* for the deploy job to work.

If the website does not update, open the failed run in the Actions tab, and
inspect the log of the step that failed. As the steps are plain shell commands,
you can reproduce nearly every failure locally from the root of the repository.


## How do I write my own tests?

The preferred way to add a test is to write it as a pytest test in `tests/` (see
\ref tarch_pytest) and to let a workflow call pytest. A minimal workflow, e.g.
`.github/workflows/pytest.yml`, reads

```
name: Python unit tests

on:
  push:
  pull_request:

jobs:
  pytest:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with:
          python-version: '3.12'
      - run: python3 -m pip install pytest
      - run: python3 -m pytest
```

Plain `pytest` runs all fast tests, which do not compile any code. Tests that
build and run Peano applications are selected through markers, e.g.
`python3 -m pytest -m integration tests/exaparty -k sod-1d`. These tests need a
compiler toolchain, and it is best to copy the setup from the documentation
workflow and to extend the `Install dependencies` step.

Some general tips:

\li A workflow is only executed if it is on the branch that triggers it, i.e. new
    workflows have to be pushed before they show up in the Actions tab.
\li Use `workflow_dispatch` as additional trigger while developing a workflow, so
    you can start it by hand.
\li Use `needs:` to make a job depend on another one, e.g. a test job on a build job.
    Jobs run on fresh machines. Pass build results between them through
    `actions/upload-artifact` and `actions/download-artifact`.
\li Pin actions to a major version (`actions/checkout@v4`) as done in the existing workflow.

*/
